#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161771 :question/id #uuid "c68d70a2-e4f8-5f08-8220-3df420a1e199" :question/math-academy-id "e-8156" :question/problem "Which of the following matrices are symmetric?\n\n\n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 1\\end{bmatrix}, \\qquad Q=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}, \\qquad R=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n" :question/worked-solution "A square matrix $A$ is symmetric if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$\nA = A^T\n$$\n\nEquivalently, $a_{ij} = a_{ji}$ for all $i,j.$ In other words, $A$ is symmetric across its main diagonal. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each matrix in turn.\n\n- $P$ is a symmetric matrix. Indeed, $P=P^T.$\n\n- $Q$ is a symmetric matrix. Indeed, $Q=Q^T.$\n\n- $R$ is *not* a symmetric matrix. Since $r_{12} \\neq r_{21},$ we obtain $R \\neq R^T.$\n\n\n$$\nR=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & {\\color{red}\\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{-2}} & -1 \\\\ {\\color{red}\\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{0}} & 2 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$P$ and $Q$ only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161816 :question/id #uuid "b159d52a-a436-5743-9151-2e147364a41d" :question/math-academy-id "e-8478" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. If $A$ is *any* $n\\times n$ matrix, then $A+A^T$ is always symmetric.\n\nII. The difference between two $n\\times n$ symmetric matrices is symmetric.\n\nIII. The product of two $n\\times n$ symmetric matrices is symmetric." :question/worked-solution "A square matrix $A$ is symmetric if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$\nA = A^T\n$$\n\nEquivalently, $a_{ij} = a_{ji}$ for all $i,j.$ In other words, $A$ is symmetric across its main diagonal.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, for any matrix $A,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A+{A}^{T})^{T} & ={A}^{T}+({A}^{T})^{T} \\\\  & ={A}^{T}+A \\\\  & =A+{A}^{T}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, the sum or difference between two symmetric matrices is symmetric.\n\n- Statement III is false. As a counterexample, consider the symmetric matrices \n\n\n$$\nA = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\quad \\text{and} \\quad B = \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n\nTheir product is not symmetric:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162586 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08e8db0d-99fb-5ea7-9318-4ca99d58e090" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P$ and $R$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354745} {:answer/id #uuid "08fcbe18-d45e-5004-92c0-3a6422d2d1cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354747} {:answer/id #uuid "828f3975-2835-5574-9c59-f895cdfa0945" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the matrices are symmetric" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355811} {:answer/id #uuid "82d2f3a8-d1ef-544d-accc-41592fb63fef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Q$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355815} {:answer/id #uuid "a7339d78-1022-501f-b997-37e63b3a3c52" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$R$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356144}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "08fcbe18-d45e-5004-92c0-3a6422d2d1cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354747} :answer-field/id #uuid "66133d70-b9fb-5d32-b845-e980e242563c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357093}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "dd69c0a4-182b-55d3-9077-6d410397ebbd" :question/math-academy-id "q-93583" :question/problem "Which of the following matrices are symmetric?\n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,Q=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 9\\end{bmatrix},\\,R=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A square matrix $A$ is symmetric if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$\nA={A}^{T}\n$$\n\nEquivalently, ${a}_{ij}={a}_{ji}$ for all $i,j.$ In other words, $A$ is symmetric across its main diagonal. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each matrix in turn.\n\n- $P$ is a symmetric matrix. Indeed, $P={P}^{T}.$\n\n- $Q$ is *not* a symmetric matrix. Since ${q}_{13}\\ne {q}_{31},$ we obtain $Q\\ne {Q}^{T}.$\n\n$$\nQ=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 9\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n- $R$ is *not* a symmetric matrix. Since ${r}_{12}\\ne {r}_{21},$ we obtain $R\\ne {R}^{T}.$\n\n$$\nR=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$P$ only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162613 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "56ba5482-5def-5eb1-8afb-b35147f01cb8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460395} {:answer/id #uuid "c2b55bdd-c3cc-58fc-a9bf-8d537f751a4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460396}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c2b55bdd-c3cc-58fc-a9bf-8d537f751a4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460396} :answer-field/id #uuid "e5900cb9-3c0a-5858-84ac-a5057863b2eb" :answer-field/key "$P=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -3 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461064} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "426fd1ef-0fc7-5598-a5b3-9ce88b4d09c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460397} {:answer/id #uuid "72b04334-21e6-5b43-a89a-7fc2f082bcfd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460398}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "426fd1ef-0fc7-5598-a5b3-9ce88b4d09c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460397} :answer-field/id #uuid "62aa287c-82c9-5b53-bffa-feef2459476d" :answer-field/key "$Q=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 4 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461065} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bc940bfb-2218-5131-b425-d004e50900ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460399} {:answer/id #uuid "97f47d98-81cc-56ed-ad89-84f36eb470e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460400}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "97f47d98-81cc-56ed-ad89-84f36eb470e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460400} :answer-field/id #uuid "00e781ce-f91c-5253-aa0a-4f69bb5ac8d2" :answer-field/key "$R=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 8 \\\\ 8 & -5\\end{bmatrix}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461066}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "4f59c753-3356-5eb7-a27a-598f27f0d76a" :question/math-academy-id "q-93994" :question/problem "Which of the following matrices are symmetric?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "A square matrix $A$ is symmetric if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$\nA={A}^{T}\n$$\n\nEquivalently, ${a}_{ij}={a}_{ji}$ for all $i,j.$ In other words, $A$ is symmetric across its main diagonal. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each matrix in turn.\n\n- $P$ is a symmetric matrix. Indeed, $P={P}^{T}.$\n\n- $Q$ is *not* a symmetric matrix. Since ${q}_{12}\\ne {q}_{21},$ we obtain $Q\\ne {Q}^{T}.$\n\n$$\nQ=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 4 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n- $R$ is a symmetric matrix. Indeed, $R={R}^{T}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$P$ and $R$ only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162676 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1dc0be98-e287-58d8-8b2c-e57d0709ae44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448822} {:answer/id #uuid "d4948f79-c7c8-5639-819a-cd61729a3714" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448823}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d4948f79-c7c8-5639-819a-cd61729a3714" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448823} :answer-field/id #uuid "5550f4ed-86c5-5c97-864d-aa737d189eeb" :answer-field/key "If $A$ is *any* matrix, then $A{A}^{T}$ is always symmetric." :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449451} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "733dd422-332d-5ac1-bd98-df4c965e888d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448824} {:answer/id #uuid "7af5d31f-43de-53e3-b3f8-80b2d1a9c9f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448825}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7af5d31f-43de-53e3-b3f8-80b2d1a9c9f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448825} :answer-field/id #uuid "b59b6c28-d8c2-5120-bb8c-d706a0e786fd" :answer-field/key "The sum of two $n\\times n$ symmetric matrices is always symmetric." :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449452} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "89550eca-a604-5392-90ab-129c47480727" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448826} {:answer/id #uuid "7ed9e463-582b-5949-bc8c-6e012d4e7b05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448827}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "89550eca-a604-5392-90ab-129c47480727" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448826} :answer-field/id #uuid "9a624701-1d1e-5fa7-ad82-6549f6ebba57" :answer-field/key "The product of two $n\\times n$ symmetric matrices is always symmetric." :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449453}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "8be226ee-498f-567c-82d1-206ed205336b" :question/math-academy-id "q-96046" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "A square matrix $A$ is symmetric if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$\nA={A}^{T}\n$$\n\nEquivalently, ${a}_{ij}={a}_{ji}$ for all $i,j.$ In other words, $A$ is symmetric across its main diagonal.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement “If $A$ is *any* matrix, then $A{A}^{T}$ is always symmetric” is true. Indeed, for any $m\\times n$ matrix $A,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A{A}^{T})^{T} & =({A}^{T})^{T}{A}^{T} \\\\  & =A{A}^{T}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “The sum of two $n\\times n$ symmetric matrices is always symmetric” is true. Indeed, for any two symmetric matrices $A$ and $B,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A+B)^{T} & ={A}^{T}+{B}^{T} \\\\  & =A+B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “The product of two $n\\times n$ symmetric matrices is always symmetric” is false. As a counterexample, consider the symmetric matrices \n\n$\\,$ $A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ \n\nHowever, their product is not symmetric:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “If $A$ is *any* matrix, then $A{A}^{T}$ is always symmetric”; “The sum of two $n\\times n$ symmetric matrices is always symmetric” only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162694 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fb469e2b-45b7-5c77-8add-fd24d81f76d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477503} {:answer/id #uuid "66140b4b-4324-5e27-a1dd-7c616d17027a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477504}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fb469e2b-45b7-5c77-8add-fd24d81f76d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477503} :answer-field/id #uuid "dbadfff9-d053-5773-a9d3-3a35eae8db62" :answer-field/key "If $A$ is *any*  $n\\times n$  matrix, then $A-{A}^{T}$ is always symmetric." :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477721} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "eb71d94b-854a-5f6f-ab43-827916efb668" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477505} {:answer/id #uuid "14acc8e7-2f06-5015-818b-6576056fa2f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477506}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "14acc8e7-2f06-5015-818b-6576056fa2f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477506} :answer-field/id #uuid "18c0a27b-9b21-5183-a539-76f7d460db30" :answer-field/key "The sum of two $n\\times n$ symmetric matrices is always symmetric." :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477722} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d1040037-a9ef-55ea-854b-01e535caaedd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477507} {:answer/id #uuid "f7b8f46f-b9ea-5dc8-9bb6-bb0919a4a4d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477508}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f7b8f46f-b9ea-5dc8-9bb6-bb0919a4a4d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477508} :answer-field/id #uuid "2734b571-73f6-5d0e-a051-dd621c984e1d" :answer-field/key "If $A$ is symmetric, then ${A}^{-1}$ is always symmetric (if it exists)." :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477723}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "2943df26-2edc-5f92-bec7-1493042a2419" :question/math-academy-id "q-96595" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "A square matrix $A$ is symmetric if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$\nA={A}^{T}\n$$\n\nEquivalently, ${a}_{ij}={a}_{ji}$ for all $i,j.$ In other words, $A$ is symmetric across its main diagonal.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement “If $A$ is *any*  $n\\times n$  matrix, then $A-{A}^{T}$ is always symmetric” is false. As a counterexample, consider the matrix \n\n$\\,$ $A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nHowever, $A-{A}^{T}$ is not symmetric:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-{A}^{T} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “The sum of two $n\\times n$ symmetric matrices is always symmetric” is true. Indeed, for any two symmetric matrices $A$ and $B,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A+B)^{T} & ={A}^{T}+{B}^{T} \\\\  & =A+B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “If $A$ is symmetric, then ${A}^{-1}$ is always symmetric (if it exists)” is true. Indeed, for any non-singular symmetric matrix $A,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({A}^{-1})^{T} & =({A}^{T})^{-1} \\\\  & ={A}^{-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “The sum of two $n\\times n$ symmetric matrices is always symmetric”; “If $A$ is symmetric, then ${A}^{-1}$ is always symmetric (if it exists)” only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272114 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12857ff7-7f34-53f2-92a8-fc5552e5223d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P$ and $R$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269431} {:answer/id #uuid "18c99cf2-a2ea-5550-8e35-29afed4e9f0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P$ and $Q$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269476} {:answer/id #uuid "555c78b8-9307-5a0d-84c0-e46552fd5cce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the matrices are symmetric" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270027} {:answer/id #uuid "c3d9d61f-bacc-5381-9864-7b41873c8c5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P,$ $Q,$ and $R$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270998} {:answer/id #uuid "c47e9ae5-65c0-542a-acd0-bd7144abd9f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Q$ and $R$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271004}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c3d9d61f-bacc-5381-9864-7b41873c8c5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P,$ $Q,$ and $R$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270998} :answer-field/id #uuid "b7ed00bf-fcba-544c-8d6c-e3b5291f4354" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271844}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "180f854f-d44c-5145-855a-5e70b4193e38" :question/math-academy-id "q-140504" :question/problem "Which of the following matrices are symmetric?\n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,Q=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & \\sqrt{3} \\\\ \\sqrt{3} & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,R=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -4 \\\\ 3 & -4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A square matrix $A$ is symmetric if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$\nA={A}^{T}\n$$\n\nEquivalently, ${a}_{ij}={a}_{ji}$ for all $i,j.$ In other words, $A$ is symmetric across its main diagonal. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each matrix in turn.\n\n- $P$ is a symmetric matrix. Indeed, $P={P}^{T}.$\n\n- $Q$ is a symmetric matrix. Indeed, $Q={Q}^{T}.$\n\n- $R$ is a symmetric matrix. Indeed, $R={R}^{T}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$P,$ $Q,$ and $R.$\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275133 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aa67ce65-42d8-53e7-8c99-76f56fc9bcce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$R$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273859} {:answer/id #uuid "bd3ce2af-19e4-5fa6-bb1c-90f4bceacb0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P$ and $R$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274024} {:answer/id #uuid "e3c641db-c970-54c3-9db3-c50ea1207234" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the matrices are symmetric" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274401} {:answer/id #uuid "e7aa91c1-4798-5ba0-80a6-7ee8a99088ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274435} {:answer/id #uuid "efe3bafc-8625-52ac-b13b-f6e88685587e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Q$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274510}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e3c641db-c970-54c3-9db3-c50ea1207234" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the matrices are symmetric" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274401} :answer-field/id #uuid "ba0e3b07-f917-5e4e-bc53-42a8b1e9f4f0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274958}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "550ab27b-8963-569e-bb9e-df189c98b4f3" :question/math-academy-id "q-143679" :question/problem "Which of the following matrices are symmetric?\n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,Q=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\sqrt{2} \\\\ 1 & 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,R=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A square matrix $A$ is symmetric if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$\nA={A}^{T}\n$$\n\nEquivalently, ${a}_{ij}={a}_{ji}$ for all $i,j.$ In other words, $A$ is symmetric across its main diagonal. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each matrix in turn.\n\n- $P$ is *not* a symmetric matrix. Since ${p}_{13}\\ne {p}_{31},$ we obtain $P\\ne {P}^{T}.$\n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n- $Q$ is *not* a symmetric matrix. Since ${q}_{13}\\ne {q}_{31},$ we obtain $Q\\ne {Q}^{T}.$\n\n$$\nQ=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\sqrt{2} \\\\ 1 & 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n- $R$ is *not* a symmetric matrix. Since ${r}_{12}\\ne {r}_{21},$ we obtain $R\\ne {R}^{T}.$\n\n$$\nR=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the matrices are symmetric.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275134 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "691d2386-08e0-5e84-8528-ca09e15fee56" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464300} {:answer/id #uuid "40fc8e5d-a7c6-5dee-ad15-ced14c3143c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464301}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "40fc8e5d-a7c6-5dee-ad15-ced14c3143c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464301} :answer-field/id #uuid "fe6f4042-36ad-5790-9b6a-ec08b4a4e1de" :answer-field/key "$P=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 11 \\\\ 2 & -1 & -9 \\\\ 11 & -9 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465084} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a166b78e-fbfa-54cb-b123-2bd64adb1d28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464302} {:answer/id #uuid "aaf7898e-f1aa-583f-936b-db61e1a09fba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464303}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aaf7898e-f1aa-583f-936b-db61e1a09fba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464303} :answer-field/id #uuid "3d8b8c80-ca14-520f-b6c2-4799187a9aeb" :answer-field/key "$Q=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465085} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d54f34b-efcc-5076-9803-a07b0f77a6b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464304} {:answer/id #uuid "7bf8d2b6-60b1-5f51-852c-b6a4df097275" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464305}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1d54f34b-efcc-5076-9803-a07b0f77a6b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464304} :answer-field/id #uuid "671893c7-dec8-5832-a276-3876dd47a359" :answer-field/key "$R=\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 7\\end{bmatrix}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465086}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "2475e8ae-4517-52f5-bd41-fe3d91352945" :question/math-academy-id "q-143680" :question/problem "Which of the following matrices are symmetric?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "A square matrix $A$ is symmetric if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$\nA={A}^{T}\n$$\n\nEquivalently, ${a}_{ij}={a}_{ji}$ for all $i,j.$ In other words, $A$ is symmetric across its main diagonal. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each matrix in turn.\n\n- $P$ is a symmetric matrix. Indeed, $P={P}^{T}.$\n\n- $Q$ is a symmetric matrix. Indeed, $Q={Q}^{T}.$\n\n- $R$ is *not* a symmetric matrix. Since ${r}_{12}\\ne {r}_{21},$ we obtain $R\\ne {R}^{T}.$\n\n$$\nR=\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 7\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$P$ and $Q$ only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275135 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "77834ff3-1b22-5a38-b89b-f82d5ab71856" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449670} {:answer/id #uuid "9b29bb6e-dcf9-528a-a29e-2d6d71349d86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449671}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9b29bb6e-dcf9-528a-a29e-2d6d71349d86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449671} :answer-field/id #uuid "a9da5bec-12d7-5de8-b874-3910e58e4788" :answer-field/key "If $A$ is a square matrix, then $A+{A}^{T}$ is always symmetric." :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450789} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7e310a01-b5b6-5b95-8ec3-f1d8a5a533b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449672} {:answer/id #uuid "fa0b9210-7a27-56eb-aa08-e1bd408b372d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449673}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fa0b9210-7a27-56eb-aa08-e1bd408b372d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449673} :answer-field/id #uuid "56acf37f-34e4-5d10-a6c2-3e5a5d140e65" :answer-field/key "If $A$ is a symmetric matrix, then ${A}^{-1}$ is always symmetric (if it exists)." :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450790} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c5b9fcc-8b52-530c-9210-d313e83c7a0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449674} {:answer/id #uuid "df70da07-5141-53a7-a1ee-97990272f70b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449675}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0c5b9fcc-8b52-530c-9210-d313e83c7a0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449674} :answer-field/id #uuid "5b6dbf8d-010e-5415-bfc1-dbb221779069" :answer-field/key "The sum of two $n\\times n$ matrices is always symmetric." :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450791}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "df88ef9d-a81d-55a1-b088-252f805046b3" :question/math-academy-id "q-143685" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "A square matrix $A$ is symmetric if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$\nA={A}^{T}\n$$\n\nEquivalently, ${a}_{ij}={a}_{ji}$ for all $i,j.$ In other words, $A$ is symmetric across its main diagonal.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement “If $A$ is a square matrix, then $A+{A}^{T}$ is always symmetric” is true. Indeed, for any square matrix $A,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A+{A}^{T})^{T} & ={A}^{T}+({A}^{T})^{T} \\\\  & ={A}^{T}+A \\\\  & =A+{A}^{T}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “If $A$ is a symmetric matrix, then ${A}^{-1}$ is always symmetric (if it exists)” is true. Indeed, for any non-singular symmetric matrix $A,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({A}^{-1})^{T} & =({A}^{T})^{-1} \\\\  & ={A}^{-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “The sum of two $n\\times n$ matrices is always symmetric” is false. As a counterexample, consider the matrices \n\n$\\,$ $A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ \n\nHowever, their sum is not symmetric:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+B & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “If $A$ is a square matrix, then $A+{A}^{T}$ is always symmetric”; “If $A$ is a symmetric matrix, then ${A}^{-1}$ is always symmetric (if it exists)” only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275136 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d290cf67-6df4-50a4-a125-89a4d83e9229" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449676} {:answer/id #uuid "7a88fac1-3096-5661-afa0-47f72ffc8f65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449677}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7a88fac1-3096-5661-afa0-47f72ffc8f65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449677} :answer-field/id #uuid "3d1092b5-ba66-50b4-8bee-d42517f8ecdf" :answer-field/key "The product of two $n\\times n$ diagonal matrices is always symmetric." :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450792} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01dc726f-0a06-5fe8-a402-62bb327452b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449678} {:answer/id #uuid "9b7f8d67-7b14-52c5-b5e2-ee93081a984b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449679}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "01dc726f-0a06-5fe8-a402-62bb327452b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449678} :answer-field/id #uuid "3b21dbc2-c542-5c91-8cc5-fb3798ea6bc5" :answer-field/key "If $A$ is *any* $n\\times n$ matrix, then $A+{A}^{2}$ is always symmetric." :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450793} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7b0c6601-486b-5c49-ae54-f084a8b9d18c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449680} {:answer/id #uuid "491b7869-9458-5892-a187-1e25f2d50b5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449681}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "491b7869-9458-5892-a187-1e25f2d50b5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449681} :answer-field/id #uuid "3f0701ff-8dbe-5272-b976-e1595f15b3ee" :answer-field/key "If $A$ is *any*  $n\\times n$ matrix, then $A{A}^{T}-{A}^{T}A$ is always symmetric." :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450794}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "416a1d82-1004-5007-b882-19d5aaa3af4e" :question/math-academy-id "q-143686" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "A square matrix $A$ is symmetric if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$\nA={A}^{T}\n$$\n\nEquivalently, ${a}_{ij}={a}_{ji}$ for all $i,j.$ In other words, $A$ is symmetric across its main diagonal.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement “The product of two $n\\times n$ diagonal matrices is always symmetric” is true. Indeed, for any diagonal matrices $A$ and $B,$ their product $AB$ is also diagonal, and any diagonal matrix is symmetric.\n\n- Statement “If $A$ is *any* $n\\times n$ matrix, then $A+{A}^{2}$ is always symmetric” is false. As a counterexample, consider the matrix \n\n$\\,$ $A=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nHowever, $A+{A}^{2}$ is not symmetric:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+{A}^{2} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “If $A$ is *any*  $n\\times n$ matrix, then $A{A}^{T}-{A}^{T}A$ is always symmetric” is true. Indeed, for any square matrix $A,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A{A}^{T}-{A}^{T}A)^{T} & =(A{A}^{T})^{T}-({A}^{T}A)^{T} \\\\  & =({A}^{T})^{T}{A}^{T}-{A}^{T}({A}^{T})^{T} \\\\  & =A{A}^{T}-{A}^{T}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “The product of two $n\\times n$ diagonal matrices is always symmetric”; “If $A$ is *any*  $n\\times n$ matrix, then $A{A}^{T}-{A}^{T}A$ is always symmetric” only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275137 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fb77d39b-75ff-5747-b99c-9cd47f41c51c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449682} {:answer/id #uuid "94345635-817a-5aa4-a7a2-498d963121ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449683}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "94345635-817a-5aa4-a7a2-498d963121ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449683} :answer-field/id #uuid "eaedc8bd-f59e-5871-bfc5-e7553143409e" :answer-field/key "The sum of two $n\\times n$ diagonal matrices is always symmetric." :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450795} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f0f84e81-499d-55d4-ac5e-99eb38720728" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449684} {:answer/id #uuid "79aeba54-2cd8-5240-8cb3-d19dcfbe4680" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449685}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f0f84e81-499d-55d4-ac5e-99eb38720728" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449684} :answer-field/id #uuid "a09ef516-6c8d-5da1-b76b-438fb19a83e7" :answer-field/key "If $A$ is *any*  $n\\times n$  matrix, then $A-{A}^{T}$ is always symmetric." :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450796} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1bcbb1cb-2004-5967-bedb-dae9a7275c83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449686} {:answer/id #uuid "2bda30dd-367b-5982-ba71-617e5ac2752d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449687}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1bcbb1cb-2004-5967-bedb-dae9a7275c83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449686} :answer-field/id #uuid "06250cb3-b876-5df2-a845-d13c4b38939b" :answer-field/key "The product of two $n\\times n$ symmetric matrices is always symmetric." :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450797}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "62cdcd34-c338-59e0-b65d-2d882ff231e6" :question/math-academy-id "q-143687" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "A square matrix $A$ is symmetric if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$\nA={A}^{T}\n$$\n\nEquivalently, ${a}_{ij}={a}_{ji}$ for all $i,j.$ In other words, $A$ is symmetric across its main diagonal.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement “The sum of two $n\\times n$ diagonal matrices is always symmetric” is true. Indeed, for any diagonal matrices $A$ and $B,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A+B)^{T} & ={A}^{T}+{B}^{T} \\\\  & =A+B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “If $A$ is *any*  $n\\times n$  matrix, then $A-{A}^{T}$ is always symmetric” is false. As a counterexample, consider the matrix \n\n$\\,$ $A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nHowever, $A-{A}^{T}$ is not symmetric:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-{A}^{T} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “The product of two $n\\times n$ symmetric matrices is always symmetric” is false. As a counterexample, consider the symmetric matrices \n\n$\\,$ $A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.$ \n\nHowever, their product is not symmetric:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “The sum of two $n\\times n$ diagonal matrices is always symmetric” only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275138 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7c7b2a77-1693-52b5-938e-64e6e1f2ceab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449688} {:answer/id #uuid "d01fdf31-92a5-57b3-8671-eed1b16afb81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449689}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d01fdf31-92a5-57b3-8671-eed1b16afb81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449689} :answer-field/id #uuid "e94131fa-ce78-5678-be60-13aa8ed6ef76" :answer-field/key "If $A$ is symmetric, then ${A}^{-1}$ is always symmetric (if it exists)." :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450798} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f8381806-836c-50a9-93cd-2a9d74f5530d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449690} {:answer/id #uuid "0d643587-3414-5f09-b4a1-16711a9473e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449691}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0d643587-3414-5f09-b4a1-16711a9473e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449691} :answer-field/id #uuid "3e188018-640d-552b-b4ec-5b8295f202cc" :answer-field/key "The sum of *any* two $n\\times n$ symmetric matrices is always symmetric." :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450799} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07f537b5-ef33-531d-bfd8-f1876dac11f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449692} {:answer/id #uuid "8087e2ff-5cd7-5646-a803-00c97f657498" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449693}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8087e2ff-5cd7-5646-a803-00c97f657498" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449693} :answer-field/id #uuid "a0930087-8fb8-5b80-8794-d9766c9702be" :answer-field/key "If $A$ is *any*  $n\\times n$ matrix, then ${A}^{T}A$ is always symmetric." :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450800}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "812925c4-9b82-5457-b638-58a29d609de3" :question/math-academy-id "q-143688" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "A square matrix $A$ is symmetric if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$\nA={A}^{T}\n$$\n\nEquivalently, ${a}_{ij}={a}_{ji}$ for all $i,j.$ In other words, $A$ is symmetric across its main diagonal.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement “If $A$ is symmetric, then ${A}^{-1}$ is always symmetric (if it exists)” is true. Indeed, for any non-singular symmetric matrix $A,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({A}^{-1})^{T} & =({A}^{T})^{-1} \\\\  & ={A}^{-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “The sum of *any* two $n\\times n$ symmetric matrices is always symmetric” is true. Indeed, for any two symmetric matrices $A$ and $B,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A+B)^{T} & ={A}^{T}+{B}^{T} \\\\  & =A+B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “If $A$ is *any*  $n\\times n$ matrix, then ${A}^{T}A$ is always symmetric” is true. Indeed, for any $m\\times n$ matrix $A,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({A}^{T}A)^{T} & ={A}^{T}({A}^{T})^{T} \\\\  & ={A}^{T}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “If $A$ is symmetric, then ${A}^{-1}$ is always symmetric (if it exists)”; “The sum of *any* two $n\\times n$ symmetric matrices is always symmetric”; “If $A$ is *any*  $n\\times n$ matrix, then ${A}^{T}A$ is always symmetric”."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357611 :question/answer-fields