id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469643}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "689daa18-2b9c-5f89-9c4a-2575e6717d8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469642} :answer-field/id #uuid "800cf770-f16d-5241-92e2-0bfe2665859c" :answer-field/key "$R=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470433}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "225f8bdf-5da4-5137-973e-d6466e5f7f7a" :question/math-academy-id "q-93985" :question/problem "Which of the following matrices are symmetric?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "A square matrix $A$ is symmetric if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$\nA={A}^{T}\n$$\n\nEquivalently, ${a}_{ij}={a}_{ji}$ for all $i,j.$ In other words, $A$ is symmetric across its main diagonal. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each matrix in turn.\n\n- $P$ is *not* a symmetric matrix. Since ${p}_{12}\\ne {p}_{21},$ we obtain $P\\ne {P}^{T}.$\n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n- $Q$ is a symmetric matrix. Indeed, $Q={Q}^{T}.$\n\n- $R$ is *not* a symmetric matrix. Since ${r}_{12}\\ne {r}_{21},$ we obtain $R\\ne {R}^{T}.$\n\n$$\nR=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$Q$ only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357612 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4ff75887-303a-5746-8422-c3fbabd4f7a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469650} {:answer/id #uuid "4211df5c-427d-5871-91ca-25355a0f4f5c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469651}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/i