=\\begin{bmatrix}7 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470439}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "cc49e39d-dc41-5d92-84ab-b1310e571077" :question/math-academy-id "q-93988" :question/problem "Which of the following matrices are symmetric?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "A square matrix $A$ is symmetric if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$\nA={A}^{T}\n$$\n\nEquivalently, ${a}_{ij}={a}_{ji}$ for all $i,j.$ In other words, $A$ is symmetric across its main diagonal. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each matrix in turn.\n\n- $P$ is a symmetric matrix. Indeed, $P={P}^{T}.$\n\n- $Q$ is *not* a symmetric matrix. Since ${q}_{12}\\ne {q}_{21},$ we obtain $Q\\ne {Q}^{T}.$\n\n$$\nQ=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n- $R$ is a symmetric matrix. Indeed, $R={R}^{T}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$P$ and $R$ only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357613 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e79675c-d340-558d-85a1-f9f7b1029de9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469662} {:answer/id #uuid "60bd1f0c-8a39-5c12-8e0f-a94ebe34fcb1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469663}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0e79675c-d340-558d-85a1-f9f7b1029de9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469662} :answer-field/id #uuid "579a2afb-cfc8-5b6a-b249-9a478ce20513" :answer-field/key "$P=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 0 & -5 \\\\ 2 & 5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470443} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7b879cd8-8163-5323-9891-635c9985bcba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469664} {:answer/id #uuid "607d0264-5580-5201-bc08-95e2af57eb0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469665}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "607d0264-5580-5201-bc08-95e2af57eb0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469665} :answer-field/id #uuid "a537cbd9-73a4-5a02-9dd4-d84d4bc3cef5" :answer-field/key "$Q=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & \\sqrt{5} & -1 \\\\ \\sqrt{5} & 0 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 3 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470444} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ff91e8eb-90ca-5476-9dd7-9c0f537bc20b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469666} {:answer/id #uuid "9efe100a-3cc8-5748-85a8-5c7f40e3cdb4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469667}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ff91e8eb-90ca-5476-9dd7-9c0f537bc20b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469666} :answer-field/id #uuid "3e526195-935e-5f64-8850-034ecc34b49e" :answer-field/key "$R=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 4 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470445}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "0a5f4b21-f8de-5f12-8b8c-c4be1021742e" :question/math-academy-id "q-93993" :question/problem "Which of the following matrices are symmetric?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "A square matrix $A$ is symmetric if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$\nA={A}^{T}\n$$\n\nEquivalently, ${a}_{ij}={a}_{ji}$ for all $i,j.$ In other words, $A$ is symmetric across its main diagonal. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each matrix in turn.\n\n- $P$ is *not* a symmetric matrix. Since ${p}_{13}\\ne {p}_{31},$ we obtain $P\\ne {P}^{T}.$\n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 0 & -5 \\\\ 2 & 5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n- $Q$ is a symmetric matrix. Indeed, $Q={Q}^{T}.$\n\n- $R$ is *not* a symmetric matrix. Since ${r}_{12}\\ne {r}_{21},$ we obtain $R\\ne {R}^{T}.$\n\n$$\nR=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 4 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$Q$ only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360684 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "61f9b779-a82b-5fca-ac7c-8326f052042f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452263} {:answer/id #uuid "e7a19fce-bfec-59e9-b174-01dd728ea53b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452264}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "61f9b779-a82b-5fca-ac7c-8326f052042f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452263} :answer-field/id #uuid "027ffc36-9391-569a-9d2f-da2a80b366dc" :answer-field/key "If $A$ is *any*  $n\\times n$ matrix, then ${A}^{2}$ is always symmetric." :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452979} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c0b1bee2-eead-5899-9436-3a5e41c5d534" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452265} {:answer/id #uuid "5c95db47-f37c-5361-aca9-caa8ca8ccc7f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452266}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c0b1bee2-eead-5899-9436-3a5e41c5d534" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452265} :answer-field/id #uuid "3698aa5e-23d2-5ab3-84e9-157fdeec0f61" :answer-field/key "The product of two $n\\times n$ symmetric matrices is always symmetric." :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452980} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b91e26b4-0173-5667-9919-d76bd3bc5172" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452267} {:answer/id #uuid "9d9e1009-c7de-59b4-8f5d-83345258f1a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452268}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9d9e1009-c7de-59b4-8f5d-83345258f1a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452268} :answer-field/id #uuid "6cfe9278-620c-5f30-9b43-3e38a539af4d" :answer-field/key "If $A$ is a symmetric matrix, then ${A}^{-1}$ is always symmetric (if it exists)." :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452981}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "a6c182c5-819c-5206-a031-9309a3557f8d" :question/math-academy-id "q-96589" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "A square matrix $A$ is symmetric if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$\nA={A}^{T}\n$$\n\nEquivalently, ${a}_{ij}={a}_{ji}$ for all $i,j.$ In other words, $A$ is symmetric across its main diagonal.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement “If $A$ is *any*  $n\\times n$ matrix, then ${A}^{2}$ is always symmetric” is false. As a counterexample, consider the matrix \n\n$\\,$ $A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nHowever, ${A}^{2}$ is not symmetric:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{2} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “The product of two $n\\times n$ symmetric matrices is always symmetric” is false. As a counterexample, consider the symmetric matrices \n\n$\\,$ $A=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ \n\nHowever, their product is not symmetric:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “If $A$ is a symmetric matrix, then ${A}^{-1}$ is always symmetric (if it exists)” is true. Indeed, for any non-singular symmetric matrix $A,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({A}^{-1})^{T} & =({A}^{T})^{-1} \\\\  & ={A}^{-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “If $A$ is a symmetric matrix, then ${A}^{-1}$ is always symmetric (if it exists)” only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360685 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f12fac1-b0d6-532b-be36-7ca114813335" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452269} {:answer/id #uuid "6b9df603-5cc7-56a3-8458-480fec1933de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452270}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6b9df603-5cc7-56a3-8458-480fec1933de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452270} :answer-field/id #uuid "cf8b0515-2015-50da-af0f-2f20ef8ea319" :answer-field/key "The product of two $n\\times n$ diagonal matrices is always symmetric." :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452982} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "753c148a-4ac7-5764-9593-da714a721e0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452271} {:answer/id #uuid "1f70874e-ec66-5ebd-8bd3-ad7a7ec28222" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452272}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "753c148a-4ac7-5764-9593-da714a721e0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452271} :answer-field/id #uuid "b9926a67-2ad5-5f8f-aa3d-cb2ad57957a6" :answer-field/key "If $A$ is *any* $n\\times n$ matrix, then ${A}^{2}$ is always symmetric." :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452983} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "62b2961b-aeac-5514-b80f-b37e49e4ab23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452273} {:answer/id #uuid "d7e4c09c-8d69-5ff3-b738-fa41770a02c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452274}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d7e4c09c-8d69-5ff3-b738-fa41770a02c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452274} :answer-field/id #uuid "e48c112e-140d-5265-8d48-23b6de6bed4a" :answer-field/key "If $A$ is *any*  $n\\times n$ matrix, then $A+{A}^{T}$ is always symmetric." :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452984}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "f89572d6-dc07-5dcb-a203-869905770fe1" :question/math-academy-id "q-96592" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "A square matrix $A$ is symmetric if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$\nA={A}^{T}\n$$\n\nEquivalently, ${a}_{ij}={a}_{ji}$ for all $i,j.$ In other words, $A$ is symmetric across its main diagonal.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement “The product of two $n\\times n$ diagonal matrices is always symmetric” is true. Indeed, for any diagonal matrices $A$ and $B,$ their product $AB$ is also diagonal, and any diagonal matrix is symmetric.\n\n- Statement “If $A$ is *any* $n\\times n$ matrix, then ${A}^{2}$ is always symmetric” is false. As a counterexample, consider the matrix \n\n$\\,$ $A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nHowever, ${A}^{2}$ is not symmetric:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{2} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “If $A$ is *any*  $n\\times n$ matrix, then $A+{A}^{T}$ is always symmetric” is true. Indeed, for any square matrix $A,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A+{A}^{T})^{T} & ={A}^{T}+({A}^{T})^{T} \\\\  & ={A}^{T}+A \\\\  & =A+{A}^{T}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “The product of two $n\\times n$ diagonal matrices is always symmetric”; “If $A$ is *any*  $n\\times n$ matrix, then $A+{A}^{T}$ is always symmetric” only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360686 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0bad97d8-ef3a-52f8-b2f7-46a5dd2b75c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452281} {:answer/id #uuid "852f50dd-59e0-589f-a1a7-fad362b65980" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :a