db/ref 17592186452285} :answer-field/id #uuid "3fd66964-e5bc-5721-9f6f-47ca70737690" :answer-field/key "If $A$ is *any*  $n\\times n$  matrix, then ${A}^{2}$ is always symmetric." :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452990}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "b61034ba-1f50-56d1-94b4-77e3ec4090fa" :question/math-academy-id "q-96596" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "A square matrix $A$ is symmetric if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$\nA={A}^{T}\n$$\n\nEquivalently, ${a}_{ij}={a}_{ji}$ for all $i,j.$ In other words, $A$ is symmetric across its main diagonal.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement “If $A$ is a symmetric matrix, then $A+{A}^{2}$ is always symmetric” is true. If $A$ is symmetric, then ${A}^{2}$ is symmetric. And, since the sum to two symmetric matrices is symmetric, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+{A}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis symmetric.\n\n- Statement “The product of *any* two $n\\times n$ matrices is always symmetric” is false. As a counterexample, consider the matrices \n\n$\\,$ $A=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ \n\nHowever, their product is not symmetric:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “If $A$ is *any*  $n\\times n$  matrix, then ${A}^{2}$ is always symmetric” is false. As a counterexample, consider the matrix \n\n$\\,$ $A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nHowever, ${A}^{2}$ is not symmetric:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{2} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “If $A$ is a symmetric matrix, then $A+{A}^{2}$ is always symmetric” only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365108 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c8b08f7a-3777-5f1a-a9eb-461a2fd0414f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451335} {:answer/id #uuid "aa1dc0f1-cd98-5509-9477-51d011494a2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451336}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c8b08f7a-3777-5f1a-a9eb-461a2fd0414f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451335} :answer-field/id #uuid "57869de9-6ee9-5e0b-90f9-6810fc1f801e" :answer-field/key "If $A$ is *any* matrix, then ${A}^{3}$ is always symmetric." :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452515} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3c06a9fc-e9e1-5f78-abd6-96a1dabad6e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451337} {:answer/id #uuid "e431efe4-4133-5a40-bbcf-62064ac9644b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451338}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e431efe4-4133-5a40-bbcf-62064ac9644b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451338} :answer-field/id #uuid "308a1332-e85b-5404-b4a5-ba01221b22ad" :answer-field/key "The sum of two $n\\times n$ symmetric matrices is always symmetric." :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452516} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9313834d-4423-5042-bc49-9390326be415" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451339} {:answer/id #uuid "28ce3ebb-bcf9-5d61-a603-60625891c0ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451340}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "28ce3ebb-bcf9-5d61-a603-60625891c0ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451340} :answer-field/id #uuid "237be3da-ec76-5d1e-936e-544f31cdde31" :answer-field/key "The difference of two $n\\times n$ symmetric matrices is always symmetric." :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452517}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "b742394a-cffb-5df9-9fbd-181d0debffeb" :question/math-academy-id "q-143683" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "A square matrix $A$ is symmetric if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$\nA={A}^{T}\n$$\n\nEquivalently, ${a}_{ij}={a}_{ji}$ for all $i,j.$ In other words, $A$ is symmetric across its main diagonal.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement “If $A$ is *any* matrix, then ${A}^{3}$ is always symmetric” is false. As a counterexample, consider the matrix \n\n$\\,$ $A=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nHowever, ${A}^{3}$ is not symmetric:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{3} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “The sum of two $n\\times n$ symmetric matrices is always symmetric” is true. Indeed, for any two symmetric matrices $A$ and $B,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A+B)^{T} & ={A}^{T}+{B}^{T} \\\\  & =A+B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “The difference of two $n\\times n$ symmetric matrices is always symmetric” is true. Indeed, for any two symmetric matrices $A$ and $B,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A-B)^{T} & ={A}^{T}-{B}^{T} \\\\  & =A-B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “The sum of two $n\\times n$ symmetric matrices is always symmetric”; “The difference of two $n\\times n$ symmetric matrices is always symmetric” only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366553 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9295980c-7c10-56c7-9256-1029bff3fede" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469644} {:answer/id #uuid "2f281361-566f-564e-90f3-dc8456c7c329" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469645}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2f281361-566f-564e-90f3-dc8456c7c329" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469645} :answer-field/id #uuid "24260372-e2e3-556d-a806-f1f44e080cec" :answer-field/key "$P=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 \\\\ 5 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470434} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "579254ab-eaa2-510b-8f52-1e585f11c777" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469646} {:answer/id #uuid "15fc8fd0-d708-5fc9-8945-923588741ef7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469647}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "15fc8fd0-d708-5fc9-8945-923588741ef7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469647} :answer-field/id #uuid "b2923d22-92c3-5826-8e93-69df978fa286" :answer-field/key "$Q=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 0.5 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0.5 & 3 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470435} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0273c12c-e70d-5a16-93e2-dabca0aa2e62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469648} {:answer/id #uuid "3790c260-bda0-5fd6-a07c-35ad33e66535" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469649}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0273c12c-e70d-5a16-93e2-dabca0aa2e62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469648} :answer-field/id #uuid "b418d0be-8006-5748-bc6f-012ee528be28" :answer-field/key "$R=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470436}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "1c1ef725-7db0-58fe-a7db-0097c019b6b6" :question/math-academy-id "q-93987" :question/problem "Which of the following matrices are symmetric?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "A square matrix $A$ is symmetric if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$\nA={A}^{T}\n$$\n\nEquivalently, ${a}_{ij}={a}_{ji}$ for all $i,j.$ In other words, $A$ is symmetric across its main diagonal. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each matrix in turn.\n\n- $P$ is a symmetric matrix. Indeed, $P={P}^{T}.$\n\n- $Q$ is a symmetric matrix. Indeed, $Q={Q}^{T}.$\n\n- $R$ is *not* a symmetric matrix. Since ${r}_{12}\\ne {r}_{21},$ we obtain $R\\ne {R}^{T}.$\n\n$$\nR=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$P$ and $Q$ only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366560 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0860eddb-8872-5a14-90cd-ea80f80450d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469656} {:answer/id #uuid "a9503884-f719-56eb-b3d2-1bbf12f445b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469657}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a9503884-f719-56eb-b3d2-1bbf12f445b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469657} :answer-field/id #uuid "2ceb7b39-d9d7-59a0-905c-bc078aea9c08" :answer-field/key "$P=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & 1\\end{bmatrix}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470440} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0316227b-bc46-5340-873c-fb4bdb297900" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469658} {:answer/id #uuid "73b30d62-76b6-5783-bbcb-a165c097c0d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469659}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0316227b-bc46-5340-873c-fb4bdb297900" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469658} :answer-field/id #uuid "e0a912f5-f285-5920-8fe7-d7356920694f" :answer-field/key "$Q=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470441} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "36ff6b0b-7b61-5a1d-b03e-95adb11dc3a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469660} {:answer/id #uuid "8c62d2d7-9891-554e-bbb7-c20ffae1d6d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469661}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "36ff6b0b-7b61-5a1d-b03e-95adb11dc3a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469660} :answer-field/id #uuid "ab17f333-c773-595b-b978-1a56da303bcf" :answer-field/key "$R=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470442}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "a97d726e-d379-5097-84d8-abb6b0a769af" :question/math-academy-id "q-93992" :question/problem "Which of the following matrices are symmetric?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "A square matrix $A$ is symmetric if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$\nA={A}^{T}\n$$\n\nEquivalently, ${a}_{ij}={a}_{ji}$ for all $i,j.$ In other words, $A$ is symmetric across its main diagonal. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each matrix in turn.\n\n- $P$ is a symmetric matrix. Indeed, $P={P}^{T}.$\n\n- $Q$ is *not* a symmetric matrix. Since ${q}_{23}\\ne {q}_{32},$ we obtain $Q\\ne {Q}^{T}.$\n\n$$\nQ=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n- $R$ is *not* a symmetric matrix. Since ${r}_{12}\\ne {r}_{21},$ we obtain $R\\ne {R}^{T}.$\n\n$$\nR=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$P$ only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366739 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ed889475-ee9c-533a-8ed7-519387cf84ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452275} {:answer/id #uuid "62ca60ad-f94f-54fa-a2aa-9d8d07b4e4cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452276}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "62ca60ad-f94f-54fa-a2aa-9d8d07b4e4cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452276} :answer-field/id #uuid "c3d3a26b-faad-53a5-854d-c0ce0d455ccd" :answer-field/key "The sum of two $n\\times n$ diagonal matrices is always symmetric." :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452985} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ecf353d3-2d53-5e63-8b1b-661cb9cb2eea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452277} {:answer/id #uuid "909b121b-5859-5703-a1e4-479d1c1bdd03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452278}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ecf353d3-2d53-5e63-8b1b-661cb9cb2eea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452277} :answer-field/id #uuid "c1c88bb5-4a8e-5849-bf4a-edd46eb5f6a3" :answer-field/key "If $A$ is *any* $n\\times n$ matrix, then ${A}^{-1}$ is always symmetric (if it exists)." :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452986} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "570d980e-c237-5c27-a69f-2dee15bb061a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452279} {:answer/id #uuid "8e4665db-639e-5f41-9895-fb286356fc79" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452280}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8e4665db-639e-5f41-9895-fb286356fc79" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452280} :answer-field/id #uuid "4c01aa01-2f35-5253-9a12-c161f9fc3268" :answer-field/key "If $A$ is *any* matrix, then ${A}^{T}A$ is always symmetric." :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452987}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "df6e5142-313f-5de3-a45d-4da4b737f0bd" :question/math-academy-id "q-96594" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "A square matrix $A$ is symmetric if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$\nA={A}^{T}\n$$\n\nEquivalently, ${a}_{ij}={a}_{ji}$ for all $i,j.$ In other words, $A$ is symmetric across its main diagonal.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement “The sum of two $n\\times n$ diagonal matrices is always symmetric” is true. Indeed, for any diagonal matrices $A$ and $B,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A+B)^{T} & ={A}^{T}+{B}^{T} \\\\  & =A+B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “If $A$ is *any* $n\\times n$ matrix, then ${A}^{-1}$ is always symmetric (if it exists)” is false. As a counterexample, consider the non-singular matrix \n\n$\\,$ $A=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nHowever, ${A}^{-1}$ is not symmetric:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “If $A$ is *any* matrix, then ${A}^{T}A$ is always symmetric” is true. Indeed, for any $m\\times n$ matrix $A,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({A}^{T}A)^{T} & ={A}^{T}({A}^{T})^{T} \\\\  & ={A}^{T}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “The sum of two $n\\times n$ diagonal matrices is always symmetric”; “If $A$ is *any* matrix, then ${A}^{T}A$ is always symmetric” only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425154 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0aa99686-720d-5c83-8792-909de2767703" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the matrices are symmetric" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425148} {:answer/id #uuid "13edd389-e8cd-52c8-8f6e-2c34ed7ad05c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P$ and $R$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425149} {:answer/id #uuid "33ef6a26-6c4e-56d3-8e19-d9dc460624f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Q$ and $R$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425150} {:answer/id #uuid "655424c1-b811-5aaf-8538-b1da76023605" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425151} {:answer/id #uuid "65dc2a15-12f3-5c0d-999e-1bc8c09548b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Q$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425152}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "33ef6a26-6c4e-56d3-8e19-d9dc460624f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Q$ and $R$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425150} :answer-field/id #uuid "2dcd0455-6677-5df3-b08a-01a2a95723e9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425153}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "ea940e2f-7cf1-5b69-9241-9cde37e5f341" :question/math-academy-id "q-140503" :question/problem "Which of the following matrices are symmetric?\n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,Q=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & \\sqrt{2} & -2 \\\\ \\sqrt{2} & -1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix},\\,R=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A square matrix $A$ is symmetric if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$\nA={A}^{T}\n$$\n\nEquivalently, ${a}_{ij}={a}_{ji}$ for all $i,j.$ In other words, $A$ is symmetric across its main diagonal. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each matrix in turn.\n\n- $P$ is *not* a symmetric matrix. Since ${p}_{12}\\ne {p}_{21},$ we obtain $P\\ne {P}^{T}.$\n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n- $Q$ is a symmetric matrix. Indeed, $Q={Q}^{T}.$\n\n- $R$ is a symmetric matrix. Indeed, $R={R}^{T}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$Q$ and $R$ only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186490230 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0088cf88-e8db-569b-9c4c-21c1044a712c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489514} {:answer/id #uuid "420f0e3f-ae88-5ea1-9342-70b2782bdeca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489665} {:answer/id #uuid "c181f6a6-71bb-5baa-8eb0-ca45258a0bd6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489934} {:answer/id #uuid "f03372e0-a80f-5ce3-be33-01eb134fd706" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490031} {:answer/id #uuid "f74b165b-f694-5acb-9521-3d180379c521" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490052}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f03372e0-a80f-5ce3-be33-01eb134fd706" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490031} :answer-field/id #uuid "eb0da733-cf1a-5325-b833-cf95bff8f2c4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490198}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "30380c91-5652-5051-8810-4fc9abb6a29f" :question/math-academy-id "q-96590" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. If $A$ is symmetric, then ${A}^{2}$ is always symmetric.\n\nII. The sum of *any* two $n\\times n$ matrices is always symmetric.\n\nIII. If $A$ is *any*  $n\\times n$ matrix, then $A+{A}^{T}$ is always symmetric." :question/worked-solution "A square matrix $A$ is symmetric if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$\nA={A}^{T}\n$$\n\nEquivalently, ${a}_{ij}={a}_{ji}$ for all $i,j.$ In other words, $A$ is symmetric across its main diagonal.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, for any symmetric matrix $A,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(AA)^{T} & ={A}^{T}{A}^{T} \\\\  & =AA.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. As a counterexample, consider the matrices \n\n$\\,$ $A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ \n\nHowever, their sum is not symmetric:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+B & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, for any square matrix $A,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A+{A}^{T})^{T} & ={A}^{T}+({A}^{T})^{T} \\\\  & ={A}^{T}+A \\\\  & =A+{A}^{T}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186490264 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1c461c57-99a8-539a-b47d-fbbf020a7142" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Q$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489582} {:answer/id #uuid "774b5e57-0730-5cba-97ab-47239dd7c8ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P$ and $R$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489790} {:answer/id #uuid "9f141855-904b-5f36-b907-0adae53edccc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the matrices are symmetric" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489868} {:answer/id #uuid "b860347c-0d9d-588a-96b1-6792b23d38ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P$ and $Q$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489921} {:answer/id #uuid "f5b378b5-639b-5712-8c19-40d90b345778" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490047}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1c461c57-99a8-539a-b47d-fbbf020a7142" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Q$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489582} :answer-field/id #uuid "0a31ec00-00a0-53b4-8edd-192981b182da" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490075}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "7243696d-a88c-5019-9844-41fc2be79db7" :question/math-academy-id "q-143682" :question/problem "Which of the following matrices are symmetric?\n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & \\frac{3}{2} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} & 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,Q=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,R=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & \\sqrt{2} & \\sqrt{3} \\\\ \\sqrt{3} & 0 & 1 \\\\ \\sqrt{2} & 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A square matrix $A$ is symmetric if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$\nA={A}^{T}\n$$\n\nEquivalently, ${a}_{ij}={a}_{ji}$ for all $i,j.$ In other words, $A$ is symmetric across its main diagonal. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each matrix in turn.\n\n- $P$ is *not* a symmetric matrix. Since ${p}_{12}\\ne {p}_{21},$ we obtain $P\\ne {P}^{T}.$\n\n$$\nQ=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & \\frac{3}{2} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n- $Q$ is a symmetric matrix. Indeed, $Q={Q}^{T}.$\n\n- $R$ is *not* a symmetric matrix. Since ${r}_{12}\\ne {r}_{21},$ we obtain $R\\ne {R}^{T}.$\n\n$$\nR=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & \\sqrt{2} & \\sqrt{3} \\\\ \\sqrt{3} & 0 & 1 \\\\ \\sqrt{2} & 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$Q$ only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186490309 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "39a0ec05-964e-5b57-9f36-6db5a9356ec3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$R$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489644} {:answer/id #uuid "673540be-83fd-5376-9753-b24c76d14c65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P,$ $Q,$ and $R$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489759} {:answer/id #uuid "6770c0a5-8a16-5c3d-9ae5-5a9d1ac80546" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Q$ and $R$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489760} {:answer/id #uuid "8dc4770d-34e5-5dc6-9c74-249ca7cf0eb6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P$ and $R$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489835} {:answer/id #uuid "d66b82c9-eba0-5b3e-a4b2-e6a29389bdaf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P$ and $Q$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489991}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "673540be-83fd-5376-9753-b24c76d14c65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P,$ $Q,$ and $R$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489759} :answer-field/id #uuid "6bfed8e4-2eb5-53f3-a595-09aab6d29d72" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490135}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "d897469f-7656-5297-824e-0adce50d0f86" :question/math-academy-id "q-93990" :question/problem "Which of the following matrices are symmetric?\n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 7\\end{bmatrix},\\,Q=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,R=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A square matrix $A$ is symmetric if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$\nA={A}^{T}\n$$\n\nEquivalently, ${a}_{ij}={a}_{ji}$ for all $i,j.$ In other words, $A$ is symmetric across its main diagonal. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each matrix in turn.\n\n- $P$ is a symmetric matrix. Indeed, $P={P}^{T}.$\n\n- $Q$ is a symmetric matrix. Indeed, $Q={Q}^{T}.$\n\n- $R$ is a symmetric matrix. Indeed, $R={R}^{T}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$P,$ $Q,$ and $R.$\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186490315 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "183f703b-ab9e-5113-9a1a-de75fd12da1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P$ and $R$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489568} {:answer/id #uuid "28596fa1-1975-520d-9ce3-c9a1b5b59843" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Q$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489606} {:answer/id #uuid "47dfbbe1-a2fb-5732-a5c5-a6f9537280bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$R$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489680} {:answer/id #uuid "bd0b36ec-7dc3-5014-a653-3ed4fb7e64e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Q$ and $R$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489928} {:answer/id #uuid "cb2e292a-4001-5d14-8fc0-0a481cb39830" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489960}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "47dfbbe1-a2fb-5732-a5c5-a6f9537280bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$R$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489680} :answer-field/id #uuid "81a5601c-846e-50b6-b119-42985d6b94c3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490149}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "e09bb1cf-2314-567d-8f07-99c777c75c71" :question/math-academy-id "q-93986" :question/problem "Which of the following matrices are symmetric?\n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,Q=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix},\\,R=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & 5 \\\\ 1 & -1 & \\sqrt{2} \\\\ 5 & \\sqrt{2} & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A square matrix $A$ is symmetric if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$\nA={A}^{T}\n$$\n\nEquivalently, ${a}_{ij}={a}_{ji}$ for all $i,j.$ In other words, $A$ is symmetric across its main diagonal. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each matrix in turn.\n\n- $P$ is *not* a symmetric matrix. Since ${p}_{12}\\ne {p}_{21},$ we obtain $P\\ne {P}^{T}.$\n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n- $Q$ is *not* a symmetric matrix. Since ${q}_{12}\\ne {q}_{21},$ we obtain $Q\\ne {Q}^{T}.$\n\n$$\nQ=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n- $R$ is a symmetric matrix. Indeed, $R={R}^{T}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$R$ only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186637564 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10cfc650-be97-5f28-b622-325c1b05a358" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186637422} {:answer/id #uuid "424c0164-928c-567f-8b99-f05dd1e036d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186637443} {:answer/id #uuid "8d81e100-0ca3-5a31-8e9c-2c38e4e8bcbc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186637476} {:answer/id #uuid "b1dbde18-3802-55a4-8d01-a16364062f8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186637492} {:answer/id #uuid "c3046871-9337-5726-8bf4-7a13a4a16018" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186637499}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "10cfc650-be97-5f28-b622-325c1b05a358" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186637422} :answer-field/id #uuid "9d7ac06a-e1c5-503f-bbe7-8cca2ecfd268" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186637542}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "784a0269-e332-516d-9a77-61711583bcb1" :question/math-academy-id "q-143689" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The difference of two $n\\times n$ diagonal matrices is always symmetric.\n\nII. If $A$ is *any* $n\\times n$ symmetric matrix, then ${A}^{T}A-A{A}^{T}$ is always symmetric.\n\nIII. The product of two $n\\times n$ symmetric matrices is always symmetric." :question/worked-solution "A square matrix $A$ is symmetric if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$\nA={A}^{T}\n$$\n\nEquivalently, ${a}_{ij}={a}_{ji}$ for all $i,j.$ In other words, $A$ is symmetric across its main diagonal.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, for any diagonal matrices $A$ and $B,$ their difference $A-B$ is also diagonal, and any diagonal matrix is symmetric.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, for any square matrix $A,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A{A}^{T}-{A}^{T}A)^{T} & =(A{A}^{T})^{T}-({A}^{T}A)^{T} \\\\  & =({A}^{T})^{T}{A}^{T}-{A}^{T}({A}^{T})^{T} \\\\  & =A{A}^{T}-{A}^{T}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. As a counterexample, consider the symmetric matrices \n\n$\\,$ $A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.$ \n\nHowever, their product is not symmetric:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186637572 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "719bc830-7c1f-5796-a7ad-c45592079676" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186637463} {:answer/id #uuid "7d68ad16-0937-5bcc-8150-f445a9477f8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186637469} {:answer/id #uuid "d7728bdd-d881-5469-ba84-681b5c3aa274" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186637507} {:answer/id #uuid "dfbb613c-26d7-531b-a5fe-3adc0aea0998" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186637509} {:answer/id #uuid "ed7455ed-1fbe-5e7a-b254-7c86390bdb2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186637518}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ed7455ed-1fbe-5e7a-b254-7c86390bdb2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186637518} :answer-field/id #uuid "17aa4888-2a66-5f6f-8119-5b0f231b3fbe" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186637527}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "d9c10879-6b44-5a9d-a860-7d921198c70d" :question/math-academy-id "q-96588" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. If $A$ is *any* matrix, then ${A}^{T}A$ is always symmetric.\n\nII. The difference between two $n\\times n$ symmetric matrices is always symmetric.\n\nIII. The product of two $n\\times n$ matrices is always symmetric." :question/worked-solution "A square matrix $A$ is symmetric if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$\nA={A}^{T}\n$$\n\nEquivalently, ${a}_{ij}={a}_{ji}$ for all $i,j.$ In other words, $A$ is symmetric across its main diagonal.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, for any $m\\times n$ matrix $A,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({A}^{T}A)^{T} & =(A)^{T}({A}^{T})^{T} \\\\  & ={A}^{T}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, for any two symmetric matrices $A$ and $B,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A-B)^{T} & ={A}^{T}-{B}^{T} \\\\  & =A-B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. As a counterexample, consider the matrices \n\n$\\,$ $A=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ \n\nHowever, their product is not symmetric:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186637575 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02f324c4-8fd8-5013-985f-7ae58b6ceb84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P$ and $Q$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186637415} {:answer/id #uuid "501e9d50-1b07-5a56-8c8a-b10bee9b7067" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186637451} {:answer/id #uuid "b17f6098-5433-51eb-8469-89729e6e98ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Q$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186637490} {:answer/id #uuid "e1070c5b-1e45-5993-b788-48c199f38933" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P$ and $R$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186637510} {:answer/id #uuid "ecc3f1e2-6e58-537d-870f-5a0a9d203b70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Q$ and $R$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186637517}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "02f324c4-8fd8-5013-985f-7ae58b6ceb84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P$ and $Q$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186637415} :answer-field/id #uuid "8b81f359-0769-5523-9e49-6fb439085800" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186637540}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "f392ffa7-fbe3-5ae1-b020-e87f723f1182" :question/math-academy-id "q-143678" :question/problem "Which of the following matrices are symmetric?\n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 5 \\\\ 2 & 5 & -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,Q=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & \\sqrt{2} & -\\sqrt{2} \\\\ \\sqrt{2} & 2 & \\sqrt{3} \\\\ -\\sqrt{2} & \\sqrt{3} & -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,R=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 7 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 7 & 3 \\\\ 7 & -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A square matrix $A$ is symmetric if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$\nA={A}^{T}\n$$\n\nEquivalently, ${a}_{ij}={a}_{ji}$ for all $i,j.$ In other words, $A$ is symmetric across its main diagonal. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each matrix in turn.\n\n- $P$ is a symmetric matrix. Indeed, $P={P}^{T}.$\n\n- $Q$ is a symmetric matrix. Indeed, $Q={Q}^{T}.$\n\n- $R$ is *not* a symmetric matrix. Since ${r}_{12}\\ne {r}_{21},$ we obtain $R\\ne {R}^{T}.$\n\n$$\nR=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 7 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 7 & 3 \\\\ 7 & -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$P$ and $Q$ only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186643116 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6544fd3b-0830-52e7-810e-1b6bd502d960" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186642790} {:answer/id #uuid "7f4ca06b-ea90-552d-957b-a946edd1a6cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186642831} {:answer/id #uuid "8f7f1998-63af-53ff-9f87-cbcce879e324" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186642852} {:answer/id #uuid "abf4f18c-8851-5266-93f4-85881b36bf01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186642897} {:answer/id #uuid "b5b3b9ba-1362-553b-9784-423474af53e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186642915}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8f7f1998-63af-53ff-9f87-cbcce879e324" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186642852} :answer-field/id #uuid "1fb78889-44f4-5a54-8e8b-d95e764dd7ca" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186643034}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "22f28ccc-1328-5b19-93a3-5108b70d92c2" :question/math-academy-id "q-96591" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. If $A$ is *any* $n\\times n$ matrix, then ${A}^{-1}$ is always symmetric (if it exists).\n\nII. The product of two $n\\times n$ symmetric matrices is always symmetric.\n\nIII. The product of two $n\\times n$ diagonal matrices is always symmetric." :question/worked-solution "A square matrix $A$ is symmetric if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$\nA={A}^{T}\n$$\n\nEquivalently, ${a}_{ij}={a}_{ji}$ for all $i,j.$ In other words, $A$ is symmetric across its main diagonal.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. As a counterexample, consider the non-singular matrix \n\n$\\,$ $A=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nHowever, ${A}^{-1}$ is not symmetric:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. As a counterexample, consider the symmetric matrices \n\n$\\,$ $A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.$ \n\nHowever, their product is not symmetric:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, for any diagonal matrices $A$ and $B,$ their product $AB$ is also diagonal, and any diagonal matrix is symmetric.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186643153 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2f9d642b-6817-56dd-a208-aaf88636f235" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186642722} {:answer/id #uuid "41cbcb0f-61b3-5213-9497-879818d85f4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186642747} {:answer/id #uuid "54b970b3-8195-5478-a37f-2c139400e065" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186642772} {:answer/id #uuid "eb3de271-46f0-51de-a749-91fe778118e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186642989} {:answer/id #uuid "eb7e68a8-5f50-5835-b6e8-f7e186350190" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186642990}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "41cbcb0f-61b3-5213-9497-879818d85f4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186642747} :answer-field/id #uuid "42aa48bb-0c98-5f6b-955c-c7b4b30586a7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186643051}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "a67aa105-a7fc-5a9e-9a5b-a3bf3fd2e741" :question/math-academy-id "q-143684" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. If $A$ is *any* matrix, then $A-{A}^{T}$ is always symmetric.\n\nII. If $A$ is *any* matrix, then $A{A}^{T}$ is always symmetric.\n\nIII. The product of two $n\\times n$ matrices is always symmetric." :question/worked-solution "A square matrix $A$ is symmetric if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$\nA={A}^{T}\n$$\n\nEquivalently, ${a}_{ij}={a}_{ji}$ for all $i,j.$ In other words, $A$ is symmetric across its main diagonal.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. As a counterexample, consider the matrix\n\n$\\,$ $A=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nHowever, $A-{A}^{T}$ is not symmetric:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-{A}^{T} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, for any $m\\times n$ matrix $A,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({A}^{T}A)^{T} & =(A)^{T}({A}^{T})^{T} \\\\  & ={A}^{T}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. As a counterexample, consider the matrices \n\n$\\,$ $A=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ \n\nHowever, their product is not symmetric:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186643185 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6c7f827d-2812-5983-a0da-712f6d09798f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P$ and $Q$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186642804} {:answer/id #uuid "b6069f86-9bd9-574f-92bf-3b5111e2cbed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P$ and $R$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186642916} {:answer/id #uuid "b94de871-2013-5b20-b81e-24726eb9b06d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P,$ $Q,$ and $R$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186642922} {:answer/id #uuid "cc851cbb-2a75-5f5d-88be-6bfce40805b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Q$ and $R$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186642951} {:answer/id #uuid "f6382061-cde5-53d5-b56a-dbbade5db691" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Q$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186643003}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cc851cbb-2a75-5f5d-88be-6bfce40805b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Q$ and $R$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186642951} :answer-field/id #uuid "b23d903b-d68f-5077-9831-be3a455e7476" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186643087}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "f09e657c-23b2-58fd-b845-9b9695b228f6" :question/math-academy-id "q-93989" :question/problem "Which of the following matrices are symmetric?\n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 3\\end{bmatrix},\\,Q=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix},\\,R=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & \\sqrt{3} \\\\ \\sqrt{3} & -4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A square matrix $A$ is symmetric if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$\nA={A}^{T}\n$$\n\nEquivalently, ${a}_{ij}={a}_{ji}$ for all $i,j.$ In other words, $A$ is symmetric across its main diagonal. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each matrix in turn.\n\n- $P$ is *not* a symmetric matrix. Since ${p}_{12}\\ne {p}_{21},$ we obtain $P\\ne {P}^{T}.$\n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n- $Q$ is a symmetric matrix. Indeed, $Q={Q}^{T}.$\n\n- $R$ is a symmetric matrix. Indeed, $R={R}^{T}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$Q$ and $R$ only.\""}]}
